WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.158
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.158 --> 00:00:10.350
Three years ago, I was sitting at
what I thought to be the perfect job,

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:16.070
I was making six figures working for
a tech company that provided free meals,

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:21.460
unlimited vacation time off and
yes, it did have a nap room.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.060
I thought I had reached
the apex of the American dream.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.657
The American dream that my
parents immigrated from China and

00:00:28.657 --> 00:00:30.427
Vietnam to this country for.

00:00:30.427 --> 00:00:35.130
And hopes that their child would have
a chance at a better life than they did.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:42.270
But it didn't feel that way,
I read and watch the news every day.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.634
Over 38 million Americans
are food insecure,

00:00:46.634 --> 00:00:51.440
children are being forcibly separated
from their families at the border.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:57.560
Texas school shooting, Florida school
shooting, voting rights under attack.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:03.720
Again, my friends and I would turn to each
other in frustration, anger and sadness.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:10.650
Did you see this,
did you hear this often I cried.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:14.488
Here I was living this picture
perfect life, but I knew back home in

00:01:14.488 --> 00:01:19.170
Philadelphia and across the country,
people were struggling just to survive.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.719
In the neighborhood my
parents first immigrated to,

00:01:23.719 --> 00:01:27.500
there are still people who can't afford
to keep a roof over their heads.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.406
Still people can't afford
to put food on the table,

00:01:32.406 --> 00:01:37.460
still children are getting asthma from
poor air quality just like I did.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.640
Still in this country we call great,
people are suffering and

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.800
dying from rooted injustices.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.440
The American Dream rang hollow.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:51.643
So, I quit my tech job,

00:01:51.643 --> 00:01:56.160
talking about these problems
wasn't going to make them go away.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.020
It wasn't enough to be frustrated,
angry or disappointed.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.632
Everything I had, that my parents
immigrated to this country for

00:02:03.632 --> 00:02:07.450
that little, if there are people
still struggling like we once did.

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:14.133
I decided to return home to Philadelphia
to do the job I had done many years ago as

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:16.460
a student, community organizing.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:22.360
To me the most urgent problem our country
face was that our democracy was in crisis.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.000
So I joined a national organization
working to get out the vote.

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.020
In that job I took a 60% pay cut and

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.630
had never been pushed to
work harder in my life.

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:38.230
We were expected to work on holidays and
to quote sleep when we are dead.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.837
It was a far cry from the comfortable
Silicon Valley life I was living just

00:02:43.837 --> 00:02:44.740
a few months ago.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.009
In that job, I met and
rediscovered community

00:02:49.009 --> 00:02:54.020
organizers who had been doing this
work for decades in Philadelphia.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:58.570
I first met Tina Marie, two years ago
working to get out the vote in Philly.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:05.120
The work we did was grueling, but it was
made even more difficult, by the pandemic.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.060
Not everyone, could vote in person,
not everyone had Internet access, and

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:13.790
the people with power and
resources didn't seem to care.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:19.317
Many times I wanted to bang my head
against the wall in frustration,

00:03:19.317 --> 00:03:22.130
but Tina Marie never gave up?

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:25.775
She showed up to meetings, she even
used her personal savings to pay for

00:03:25.775 --> 00:03:27.550
her neighbor's internet costs.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:34.250
Tina Marie is over 60 now, but
she has the energy of a teenager.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:39.198
For over four decades she has been serving
her community, she spends her Wednesdays

00:03:39.198 --> 00:03:43.690
attending zoning board meetings,
which can go on for six hours.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:48.321
She holds countless meetings for her
neighbors to hold developers accountable,

00:03:48.321 --> 00:03:52.630
demanding that they invest in home
ownership, not just profit off of rent.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.712
Tina Marie does this
work around the clock,

00:03:56.712 --> 00:04:01.710
she works every day into the evenings and
on the weekends to serve her community.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:10.020
And she does this work for free,
yes, you heard that correctly.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:13.223
Tina Marie doesn't have
a job that pays her,

00:04:13.223 --> 00:04:16.269
serving her community is a full time role.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.730
Why does she do it, you may ask?

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:25.038
Because she says, if she doesn't do it,
no one else will.

00:04:25.038 --> 00:04:29.805
Her neighborhood, like many
marginalized neighborhoods across

00:04:29.805 --> 00:04:34.060
the country have faced
the same problems for decades.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:40.343
Lack of quality housing Education, jobs,
imagine living paycheck to paycheck,

00:04:40.343 --> 00:04:43.844
sending your kids to
schools with asbestos and

00:04:43.844 --> 00:04:48.170
being surrounded by vacant
lots overfilled with trash.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:54.650
That's a reality that many Americans in
this country still have to face today.

00:04:55.790 --> 00:05:00.130
And so you have people like Tina Marie,
and 10s of 1000s of others,

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:05.100
fighting for their communities around the
clock without pay and with little rests,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.610
despite all they have endured,
they're still giving back.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:15.578
They won't wait why, because they're
fighting for something deeper than money,

00:05:15.578 --> 00:05:19.540
prestige or power,
they're fighting for their home.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:24.410
When his neighbors didn't know
how to vote during the pandemic,

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:28.060
Maurice co-founded
the Philly Voter Engagement Network.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:32.121
He built a team of hundreds of people
working to spread the vote about mail and

00:05:32.121 --> 00:05:34.480
ballot options and polling place changes.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:41.248
When not enough was done to educate
students about voting Tom co founded pa

00:05:41.248 --> 00:05:42.720
youth vote.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.323
He built a coalition of teachers in
more than 40 schools to become voter

00:05:47.323 --> 00:05:51.180
champions and empower their
students to take civic action.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:55.947
When nothing was done about gun
violence in her neighborhood,

00:05:55.947 --> 00:05:58.610
Kendra started stand up nice town.

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:01.875
She brought a new resource to our
community every week to change

00:06:01.875 --> 00:06:05.830
the conversation, like job readiness
training and counseling services.

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:11.077
When an outside entity tried to take
over her neighborhood public school,

00:06:11.077 --> 00:06:15.160
tend to galvanize parents to vote for
it to be kept in the public's control.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:20.178
And it is through community organizing,
that she won her seat and

00:06:20.178 --> 00:06:23.440
city council I'm still fighting for
justice for her home.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:33.030
Community Organizing is treated as
volunteer work, but it shouldn't be.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.811
It's why we have a five day work week,
it's why we have paid time off,

00:06:38.811 --> 00:06:42.280
it's why everyone in this
room has the right to vote.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:48.080
As we stand here today, because
community organizers fought for us.

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:54.657
When this country was founded,
there was no concept of overtime pay or

00:06:54.657 --> 00:06:57.894
minimum wage child labor was not uncommon.

00:06:57.894 --> 00:07:03.458
For people unionized and fought,
organizing major strikes to demand better.

00:07:03.458 --> 00:07:08.339
Their actions built the momentum for
the Fair Labor Standards Act,

00:07:08.339 --> 00:07:13.919
banning oppressive child labor and
instituting the workweek we have today.

00:07:13.919 --> 00:07:19.632
It's time we stop taking community
organizing for granted and pay it forward.

00:07:19.632 --> 00:07:25.031
I believe in people power I believe
that we need more grassroots leaders,

00:07:25.031 --> 00:07:28.560
who are from the communities they serve.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.175
People like Tina Marie,
Tom, Kendra, and Maurice,

00:07:32.175 --> 00:07:35.720
people who won't give up
because this is their home.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:41.368
Community Organizing is often treated
as volunteer work, like I said, but

00:07:41.368 --> 00:07:42.330
it shouldn't be.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:47.650
We should really focus on centering these
people in the movement for justice.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:55.160
It's easy to become complacent, to feel
like everything is perfect, to do nothing.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:01.826
But I'm asking you for just a few
minutes to think about where you sit

00:08:01.826 --> 00:08:07.180
in this world even right now here at
Stanford in this very room today.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:17.210
Take a look around we are sitting in an
institution with a $37 billion endowment.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:22.078
Stanford's highest paid
employee makes over $8 million

00:08:22.078 --> 00:08:26.456
hugging the western edge of
campus is sand hill road,

00:08:26.456 --> 00:08:33.190
the epicenter of venture capital and the
most expensive street in all of America.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:40.554
At the same time on El Camino rial, which
hugs the north eastern edge of campus,

00:08:40.554 --> 00:08:45.920
dozens of Arby's are parked filled
with people just trying to get by.

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:53.034
Abigail couldn't afford the $600
increase from her landlord in rent,

00:08:53.034 --> 00:08:56.040
so she and
her family of 10 moved into an RV.

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:01.519
Tamika and Ivan moved into an RV
when the city started cracking down

00:09:01.519 --> 00:09:03.620
on people living in cars.

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:07.580
Despite both being employed,
they couldn't afford the rent.

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:12.712
What may look perfect
from your vantage point,

00:09:12.712 --> 00:09:16.270
looks like how to someone who
doesn't have that privilege.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:21.340
We were all raised to believe
in the American dream,

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.970
but what does the American dream mean?

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:30.985
If we can't all have it what does
it mean when the barriers are so

00:09:30.985 --> 00:09:35.977
high only a few can access it and
what does it mean when

00:09:35.977 --> 00:09:40.993
we continue to always leave
the same people behind.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:47.670
Community organizers are fighting for
affordable housing, generational

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:52.300
wealth and voting rights because they
know that not everyone has access to it.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:58.298
Living means being a part
of the world you live in,

00:09:58.298 --> 00:10:03.784
it means taking care of each other,
it means standing up for

00:10:03.784 --> 00:10:08.190
each other,
especially when no one else will.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:12.200
That is what the American
dream should look like.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:19.172
And so I'm asking you today to care
to make it better to advocate for

00:10:19.172 --> 00:10:24.276
invested and
center the voices of community organizers,

00:10:24.276 --> 00:10:28.251
like Tina Marie, Tom, Maurice and Kendra.

00:10:28.251 --> 00:10:33.501
It's not about any one of
us it's about all of us,

00:10:33.501 --> 00:10:37.980
it's about what makes us human.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:42.986
Caring for each other so
let's get to work together.

00:10:42.986 --> 00:10:46.646
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:46.646 --> 00:10:48.672
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

